            <ArticleTitle>Mitochondrial uncoupling protein 2 (UCP2) gene polymorphisms are associated with childhood obesity and related metabolic disorders.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>277-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/jpem-2012-0267</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/jpem.2013.26.issue-3-4/jpem-2012-0267/jpem-2012-0267.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study aimed to investigate the possible role of uncoupling protein 2 (UCP2) gene polymorphisms in childhood obesity and related metabolic disorders.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Obese patients (n=100) and healthy controls (n=100) were analyzed for -866G&gt;A and insertion/deletion (I/D) polymorphisms of the UCP2 gene by polymerase chain raction and/or restriction fragment length polymorphism.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">UCP2 I/D polymorphism showed an association with obesity. The insertion homozygous genotype (II) was higher in obese patients (p=0.0001), while the DD genotype was higher in controls (p=0.0034). Body mass index and relative weight were lower in patients carrying the A allele of the -866G&gt;A polymorphism (p=0.021 and p=0.047, respectively). There was an association between insulin resistance and -866A allele carrier patients with consanguineous parents (p=0.005).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Insertion homozygous genotype and the allele of I/D polymorphism were found to be risk factors for childhood obesity and related metabolic disorders. The -866A allele was associated with susceptibility to central adiposity, hypercholesterolemia, hypertriglyceridemia and insulin resistance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
